		  <div style="opacity:0" class="sc-bCwfaz hzzSzX __pf" id="__pf"><div data-pf-type="Body" class="sc-iNiQyp jdiWPe pf-1_"><div data-pf-type="Layout" class="sc-hTRkXV enlJuL pf-2_"><div data-pf-type="Section" class="sc-lbVvki ghCdOs pf-3_"><div style="--cw:1108px" class="sc-eirqVv dSzotL"><div class="sc-iwajpm kydRHc pf-4_ pf-bg-lazy pf-r pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:15px" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:6;--c-md:5;--c-lg:5"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-jffHpj jjTcTs pf-5_"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0574/5901/5852/t/8/assets/pf-e71e94bf--100yearanniversary-1_365x.gif?v=1625331218" srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0574/5901/5852/t/8/assets/pf-e71e94bf--100yearanniversary-1.gif?v=1625331218 1536w" width="365" height="331" loading="lazy" data-pf-type="Image" class="sc-ciSkZP bwSYJA pf-6_"/></div></div><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:6;--c-md:7;--c-lg:7"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-jffHpj jjTcTs pf-7_"><h1 data-pf-type="Heading" class="sc-jJMGnK evXokT pf-8_"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-10_"><span style="font-weight: bold;"><br>The History of <span style="color: rgb(218, 165, 32);">Hodges</span> Badges Company<br><span style="color: rgb(218, 165, 32);">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 1920s</span><br></span></span></h1></div></div></div><div class="sc-iwajpm kydRHc pf-11_ pf-bg-lazy pf-r pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:15px" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:6;--c-lg:6"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-jffHpj jjTcTs pf-12_"><p class="sc-fXazdy ujFwY pf-13_" data-pf-type="Paragraph"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-15_"><span style="font-weight: bold;"><span style="color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">1920s: Where it all began.&nbsp;</span><br></span><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">It was an unusually chilly morning in Milford, New Hampshire on September 18, 1920. The sun was bright, and a proud Willian Hodges stood in front of a red brick building with utter excitement. This was the beginning, where it all started, Hodges Badge founded by William Hodges.</span><br><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">William worked for another badge company that just recently closed its doors due to financial hardships, now stood unemployed for the first time in his life. With a family to feed, a dream and seeing the possibilities in front of him, purchased a printing press from his now defunked employer to start his own business. With much resistance from his wife and children, he forged forward with a promise to his family, that he would succeed. All he requested from them, was to be supportive in the beginning days. William would work long hours printing the ribbons, while his two daughters, Anna Marice and Emaline Genevive, stitched the ribbons. Word traveled fast that Hodges Badge was a reliable quality business to purchase ribbons. Business was booming.</span><br><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Many big events happened in the 1920s, better known as the “Roaring Twenties”. Social and political changes were all the topic at every event in the United States. Many more Americans were now living in cities. The nation’s economic growth more than doubled. Hodges Badge Company moved to the Greater Boston Area into a two-room loft space at 166 Essex Street Boston, MA. Frederick J. Hodges, William’s son and his oldest child, joined the company as a full-time employee in 1924. Frederick left his job at the Boston Marine Railroad. The company was growing faster than William could have ever imagined.</span><br><br><br><br></span></p><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0574/5901/5852/t/8/assets/pf-d270bd83--hbc1900srosettesbw_500x.jpg?v=1625232424" srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0574/5901/5852/t/8/assets/pf-d270bd83--hbc1900srosettesbw.jpg?v=1625232424 1536w, https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0574/5901/5852/t/8/assets/pf-d270bd83--hbc1900srosettesbw_500x.jpg?v=1625232424 320w" width="515" height="442" loading="lazy" data-pf-type="Image" class="sc-ciSkZP bwSYJA pf-16_"/><p class="sc-fXazdy ujFwY pf-17_" data-pf-type="Paragraph"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-19_"><span style="font-style: italic;">The rosettes depicted are from the badge company that William Hodges worked for before he started Hodges Badge Company. These rosettes are still proudly displayed at the headquarters in Portsmouth, RI.</span></span></p></div></div><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:6;--c-lg:6"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-jffHpj jjTcTs pf-20_"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0574/5901/5852/t/8/assets/pf-591293f4--166essexboston1920_500x.jpg?v=1625331804" srcSet="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0574/5901/5852/t/8/assets/pf-591293f4--166essexboston1920.jpg?v=1625331804 1536w, https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0574/5901/5852/t/8/assets/pf-591293f4--166essexboston1920_500x.jpg?v=1625331804 320w" width="515" height="657" loading="lazy" data-pf-type="Image" class="sc-ciSkZP bwSYJA pf-21_"/><p class="sc-fXazdy ujFwY pf-22_" data-pf-type="Paragraph"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-24_"><span style="font-style: italic;">(“Buildings South Side of Essex St., Buildings East Side Washington St., Boston, Mass., Undated.”)
Pictured are office buildings on Essex Street, Boston, MA, in the mid-1920s. Hodges Badge Company’s first location in Boston, was located at 166 Essex Street Boston, MA, and is shown among these buildings.</span><br><br><span style="font-style: italic;">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</span><span style="font-weight: bold;"><span style="font-style: italic;">&nbsp;</span></span><br></span></p><ul style="--text-indent:0;--line-spacing:10px" data-pf-type="List" class="sc-ckTSus kVjkXt pf-25_"><li data-pf-type="List.Item" class="sc-fbIWvP jREztg pf-26_"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-28_">The Roaring '20s were a time of a booming stock market, speakeasies, short skirts, the Charleston, and jazz.</span></li><li data-pf-type="List.Item" class="sc-fbIWvP jREztg pf-29_"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-31_">Women won the right to vote.</span></li><li data-pf-type="List.Item" class="sc-fbIWvP jREztg pf-32_"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-34_">World War I ended.</span></li><li data-pf-type="List.Item" class="sc-fbIWvP jREztg pf-35_"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-37_">Babe Ruth hit his home run record of 60 home runs.</span></li><li data-pf-type="List.Item" class="sc-fbIWvP jREztg pf-38_"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-40_">Mickey and Minnie Mouse first appeared in a film.</span></li><li data-pf-type="List.Item" class="sc-fbIWvP jREztg pf-41_"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-43_">The American Professional Football League was formed.</span></li><li data-pf-type="List.Item" class="sc-fbIWvP jREztg pf-44_"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="sc-gsWcmt grZHMV pf-46_">Amelia Earhart became the first woman to fly over the Atlantic Ocean.</span></li></ul><a href="https://hodges-badge-dev.myshopify.com/a/pf_preview?id=657c52e3-5126-4d53-a5c1-9e96de30c206" target="_self" data-pf-type="Button" class="sc-cxNHIi jnA-diB pf-47_"><span data-pf-type="Text" class="s